<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Musa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Musa is an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> near the Strait of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a>, long administered by Sharjah. Under a memorandum of understanding of November 1971 Iranian troops were stationed on part of it while Sharjah kept administration of the rest. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> extended its control over the whole island in 1992, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> remains disputed between Iran and the United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-musa.jpg" alt="Abu Musa" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pascal · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Strait_of_Hormuz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Abu Musa is an island of 12.8 square kilometres in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, near the entrance to the Strait of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a>. Its highest point is Mount Halva, at 110 metres. Because of the depth of the sea, oil tankers and other large ships have to pass between Abu Musa and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-and-lesser-tunbs">Greater and Lesser Tunbs</a>, which makes the islands one of the strategic points of the Gulf. The climate is hot and humid, the soil and water are poor for farming, and fishing is the main local industry. Arabic sources derive the name from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, a companion of Muhammad who stayed there in 643 CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> region, Abu Musa included, in the pre-Islamic era. In the early Islamic period the island was ruled by the Umayyad and Abbasid caliphates until the Iranian Buyid dynasty conquered it in 935. Local rulers of southern Iran later managed these islands and ports with varying independence. A 13th-century account by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqut-al-hamawi">Yaqut al-Hamawi</a> gives what may be the first mention of the island. The Portuguese captured it in the 16th century and Iran retook it in 1622.</p><p>By common consent the island was later under the Al-Qasimi ruler of Sharjah and was covered by a protection treaty between the Gulf rulers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> of 1819. The first recorded dispute came in 1904, when Iranians replaced a flag of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trucial-states">Trucial States</a> with their own, and British authorities opposed this. In 1964 Iranian forces occupied it for 20 days.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> announced in 1968 that it would leave the Gulf, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> moved to attach the island politically to the mainland. On 30 November 1971 Iran and Sharjah signed a memorandum of understanding that let Iranian troops be stationed on the island and left Sharjah a police station, and divided the island's energy resources. The United Arab Emirates took its claim to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-security-council">United Nations Security Council</a> on 9 December 1971, which deferred consideration of the matter. In 1992 Iran expelled foreign workers who ran a school, clinic and power station sponsored by the UAE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The island is administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> as part of Abumusa County in Hormozgan province, and the United Arab Emirates claims it as part of its Sharjah emirate. Abu Musa city is the island's centre. Most native residents speak the Bandari dialect of Persian, and the island has a regional airport.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abu Musa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Iran claimed the island of <b>Abu Musa</b>, which belonged to Sharjah, and in November 1971 Khalid accepted a memorandum of understanding under which Iranian troops were stationed on part of the island while Sharjah kept administration of the rest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sheikhdom-of-sharjah">Sheikhdom of Sharjah · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On the eve of independence Iran occupied the Greater and Lesser Tunbs, claimed by Ras al-Khaimah, and landed troops on <b>Abu Musa</b> under an agreement with Sharjah; the UAE still disputes all three islands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-arab-emirates">United Arab Emirates · Military</a></li></ul></section>
